<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 7: Handling Factors                                      -->
<!--    Checked over and happy with it (for now at least!)             -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-handling-factors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Handling Factors</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-handling-factors">
    <p>
      In 1950, the South African government made a decision that would shape the
      lives of millions of people for decades.
      It decided to write down, officially and legally, what race everyone was.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this chapter, we're going to look carefully at variables like this one:
      variables that take a fixed set of named values.
      With these variables, the choice of what those values are, and how they're
      defined, affects every analysis that comes after.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: When a category changes the data                      -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-population-group-factors">
    <title>When a Category Changes the Data</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The story of <q>population group</q> shows how a single variable can
        shape an entire system of laws, and how that system can change over
        time.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-factors-legal-classification">
      <title>A classification imposed by law</title>

      <p>
        In 1950, the South African government passed the Population Registration
        Act, which required every person living in South Africa to be officially
        classified into a racial category.
        At its simplest, the Act set up three categories: White, Coloured, and
        Native (also called <q>Bantu</q>).
        Indians, the descendants of South Asians who had come to South Africa
        under British rule, didn't get a category of their own at first.
        As we'll see, they were placed within the Coloured category, and it was
        only later that a fourth category, Asian, was added, on the stated
        grounds that Indians had <q>no historical right to the country</q>.
      </p>

      <p>
        And the classification system didn't stop there.
        Within each of these top-level categories, the law allowed, and
        eventually required, further subdivisions.
        By 1959, the Coloured category alone had been split into seven
        subgroups: Cape Coloured, Cape Malay, Griqua, Chinese, Indian, Other
        Asiatic, and Other Coloured.
        People were assigned to these groups based on their physical appearance,
        how they were generally seen in their community, and their descent.
        In other words, the same person could, in principle, be classified
        differently depending on who was doing the classifying.
      </p>

      <p>
        This racial classification became the foundation for a whole system of
        laws.
        The Group Areas Act decided where people in each category were allowed
        to live.
        The Bantu Authorities Act restructured government for people classified
        as Native, putting authority in the hands of traditional tribal leaders
        within designated homelands.
        The Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act made it illegal for a White
        person to marry someone from another classification.
        The Immorality Amendment Act went even further, and made sexual
        relations between a White person and anyone from another classification
        a crime.
        A person's most basic rights and opportunities (where they lived, who
        they could marry, which schools their children went to, and what work
        they could do) all came from a single categorical variable, assigned at
        birth.
      </p>

      <p>
        We start this chapter on factors here because this is one of the
        starkest examples in history of what it means to reduce a human being to
        a category, and of the very real consequences that categorical variables
        can carry.
      </p>

      <p>
        In statistics, we call this kind of variable a <term>factor</term>: a
        variable that takes one of a fixed set of values, where each of those
        values is called a <term>level</term>.
        The Population Registration Act created a factor with a small number of
        levels, defined by law, and then built an entire system on top of it.
        In data analysis, the stakes are rarely that high, but the principle is
        the same.
        The levels you choose, and how you define them, shape every summary,
        every comparison, and every model that follows.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Population Registration Act, 1950 (Act No.
        30 of 1950), and Population Registration Act Repeal Act, 1991 (Act No.
        114 of 1991), Parliament of South Africa.
        South Africa Population Census 1980, population-group variable codebook,
        World Bank Microdata Library.
        Khalfani, A.A.
        and Zuberi, T.
        (2001).
        <q>Racial
        classification and the modern census in South Africa,
        1911<ndash/>1996.</q>
        <em>Race and Society</em>, 4(2), 161 <ndash/> 176.
        Statistics South Africa (2023).
        <em>Census 2022: Statistical
        Release P0301.4.</em> Pretoria:
        Stats SA.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

