  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 2: ordered vs. unordered, and when to factor at all       -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-factors-ordered-vs-unordered">
    <title>The Power of Factors using R</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Factors come in two kinds.
        The first kind is <term>unordered factors</term>, where the levels are
        just different categories, with no natural ranking between them.
        The racial classifications of the Population Registration Act are one
        example: <q>White</q> doesn't come before or after <q>Coloured</q> in
        any meaningful order.
        Continent is another: <q>Africa</q>, <q>Asia</q>, <q>Europe</q>, and
        <q>the Americas</q> are different from each other, but none of them is
        greater or lesser than another.
        In data analysis, most categorical variables are unordered factors like
        these.
      </p>

      <p>
        The second kind is <term>ordered factors</term>, where the levels have a
        natural order that carries real meaning.
        Kenya's Competency Based Curriculum (CBC) has really clear ordered
        levels: <q>Pre-Primary</q>, <q>Lower Primary</q>, <q>Upper Primary</q>,
        <q>Junior Secondary</q>, and <q>Senior Secondary</q>.
        When researchers and policymakers analyse educational outcomes, that
        order matters.
        A student in Upper Primary is in a different group from a student in
        Junior Secondary, and they're also at an earlier stage in a meaningful
        progression.
        The order within a factor can, and should, inform how we summarise and
        analyse the data.
      </p>

      <p>
        Humanitarian data gives us an even starker example.
        The Integrated Phase Classification (IPC) system, used across Kenya and
        the wider East Africa region, puts food security into five ordered
        levels: <q>Minimal</q>, <q>Stressed</q>, <q>Crisis</q>,
        <q>Emergency</q>, and <q>Famine</q>.
        Governments and international agencies use this classification to track
        food shortages, malnutrition, and death rates, so they can direct
        emergency aid.
        And the order really matters: Crisis is meaningfully worse than
        Stressed, and Emergency is meaningfully worse than Crisis.
        If an analyst treated these five levels as an unordered factor, any
        summary or comparison they produced would be misleading at best.
        At worst, it could inform decisions that cost lives.
      </p>

      <p>
        In R, the tools for working with factors are built into the base
        language, and extended by the <c>forcats</c> package (part of the
        tidyverse).
        Before you work with a factor, there are three basic checks worth
        knowing:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      library(dplyr)
      library(forcats)
      library(readxl)
      library(lubridate)

      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- read_excel("kenya-accidents-database.xlsx")

      # Is VICTIM already a factor?
      is.factor(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt; [1] FALSE

      # Convert it to one
      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        mutate(VICTIM = as.factor(VICTIM))

      is.factor(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt; [1] TRUE

      # What are its levels?
      # (Note that we are assuming this data is not cleaned yet,
      # so there are some typos and duplicates in the levels.
      # This can be another approach to data cleaning to look
      # at the levels to then fix them!)
      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt; [1] "2 PASSENGERS &amp; DRIVER"       "2 PEDESTRIANS &amp; PASSENGER"  
      #&gt; [3] "4 PASSENGER 1 DRIVER"        "7 PASSENGERS 1 DRIVER"      
      #&gt; [5] "DRIVER"                      "DRIVER PASSENGER"           
      #&gt; [7] "M/CYCLIST"                   "M/CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER"    
      #&gt; [9] "MOTOR CYCLIST"               "MOTOR CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER"
      #&gt; [11] "MOTOR CYCLIST P/PASSENGER"   "P/CYCLIST"                  
      #&gt; [13] "P/PASENGER"                  "P/PASSENGER"                
      #&gt; [15] "PASSENGER"                   "PASSENGERS"                 
      #&gt; [17] "PEDAL CYCLIST"               "PEDESTRIAN"                 
      #&gt; [19] "RIDER"

      # Is it ordered?
      is.ordered(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt; [1] FALSE
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        <c>VICTIM</c> is an unordered factor, because there's no sensible way to
        rank pedestrians, drivers, and passengers.
        But now let's pull the month out of the date column.
        Months have a clear order that everyone agrees on: January comes before
        February, and February comes before March.
        So we can make month an ordered factor:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        mutate(
          month = lubridate::month(`Date DD/MM/YYYY`, label = TRUE)
        )

      # What does this give us?
      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$month)
      #&gt; [1] "Jan" "Feb" "Mar" "Apr" "May" "Jun" "Jul" "Aug" "Sep" "Oct" "Nov" "Dec"

      is.ordered(kenya_accidents_2016$month)
      #&gt; [1] TRUE
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        <c>lubridate::month(label = TRUE)</c> gives us month as an ordered
        factor automatically, so R knows that April comes before June.
        We can check that R is really using this calendar order, and not
        alphabetical order, by comparing June with December.
        Alphabetically, <q>Jun</q> comes after <q>Dec</q>, but in the calendar,
        June comes first:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # Take the month of the first June crash
      june_crash &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016$month[
        which(kenya_accidents_2016$month == "Jun")[1]
      ]

      # As an ordered factor, June comes before December
      june_crash &lt; "Dec"
      #&gt; [1] TRUE

      # But as plain text, "Jun" comes after "Dec" alphabetically
      as.character(june_crash) &lt; "Dec"
      #&gt; [1] FALSE
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Ordered factors also let you calculate some statistics that don't make
        sense for unordered factors.
        For example, the earliest or latest month in a dataset means something.
        If a dataset has January, February, and March in it, the minimum month
        is January, and the maximum month is March.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      min(kenya_accidents_2016$month)
      #&gt; [1] Apr
      #&gt; 12 Levels: Jan &lt; Feb &lt; Mar &lt; Apr &lt; May &lt; Jun &lt; Jul &lt; Aug &lt; Sep &lt; Oct &lt; ... &lt; Dec

      max(kenya_accidents_2016$month)
      #&gt; [1] Jun
      #&gt; 12 Levels: Jan &lt; Feb &lt; Mar &lt; Apr &lt; May &lt; Jun &lt; Jul &lt; Aug &lt; Sep &lt; Oct &lt; ... &lt; Dec
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        If we want to change the order of an unordered factor's levels (for
        example, to control the order they appear in a plot), we can use
        <c>fct_relevel()</c> from the <c>forcats</c> package to set the order
        ourselves:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # Default level order for VICTIM is alphabetical
      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt;  [1] "2 PASSENGERS &amp; DRIVER"       "2 PEDESTRIANS &amp; PASSENGER"
      #&gt;  [3] "4 PASSENGER 1 DRIVER"        "7 PASSENGERS 1 DRIVER"
      #&gt;  [5] "DRIVER"                      "DRIVER PASSENGER"
      #&gt;  [7] "M/CYCLIST"                   "M/CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER"
      #&gt;  [9] "MOTOR CYCLIST"               "MOTOR CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER"
      #&gt; [11] "MOTOR CYCLIST P/PASSENGER"   "P/CYCLIST"
      #&gt; [13] "P/PASENGER"                  "P/PASSENGER"
      #&gt; [15] "PASSENGER"                   "PASSENGERS"
      #&gt; [17] "PEDAL CYCLIST"               "PEDESTRIAN"
      #&gt; [19] "RIDER"

      # Reorder to put PEDESTRIAN first
      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        mutate(VICTIM = fct_relevel(VICTIM, "PEDESTRIAN", "PASSENGER",
                                    "DRIVER", "M/CYCLIST", 
                                    "P/PASSENGER", "P/CYCLIST"))

      # Check the new order
      # Note that the levels not mentioned in `fct_relevel()` are still there,
      # but now come after the ones we specified
      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
      #&gt;  [1] "PEDESTRIAN"                  "PASSENGER"                  
      #&gt;  [3] "DRIVER"                      "M/CYCLIST"                  
      #&gt;  [5] "P/PASSENGER"                 "P/CYCLIST"                  
      #&gt;  [7] "2 PASSENGERS &amp; DRIVER"       "2 PEDESTRIANS &amp; PASSENGER"  
      #&gt;  [9] "4 PASSENGER 1 DRIVER"        "7 PASSENGERS 1 DRIVER"      
      #&gt; [11] "DRIVER PASSENGER"            "M/CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER"    
      #&gt; [13] "MOTOR CYCLIST"               "MOTOR CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER"
      #&gt; [15] "MOTOR CYCLIST P/PASSENGER"   "P/PASENGER"                 
      #&gt; [17] "PASSENGERS"                  "PEDAL CYCLIST"              
      #&gt; [19] "RIDER"
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> World Hunger Education Service.
        (2024, December).
        Famine fact sheet.
        https://www.worldhunger.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Famine-Fact-Sheet-Dec-2024.pdf
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-factors-numeric-or-factor">
      <title>Numeric, unordered, or ordered: it is a judgement call</title>

      <p>
        Variables don't always tell you what they are.
        A column of numbers might be a quantity you can add up and average, or
        it might be a label that just happens to be written as a number.
        A column of text might be a free-form description, or it might be one of
        a fixed set of categories.
        The data itself doesn't always tell you which.
        That's a judgement you have to make, and it affects every analysis that
        follows.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <c>CAUSE CODE</c> column in the Kenya Road Accidents data is a good
        example.
        It contains numbers (7, 25, 98, 26, 63), and R reads it in as numeric by
        default.
        But these numbers aren't quantities.
        They're administrative codes for different categories of crash cause.
        Code 7 means one thing, and code 25 means another.
        Code 50 isn't halfway between code 25 and code 75, and the average cause
        code for a set of crashes tells you nothing useful.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # R reads CAUSE CODE as numeric
      class(kenya_accidents_2016$`CAUSE CODE`)
      #&gt; [1] "numeric"

      # This lets R do arithmetic on it - but should it?
      mean(kenya_accidents_2016$`CAUSE CODE`, na.rm = TRUE)
      #&gt; [1] 47.430...

      # The mean cause code is meaningless. Convert to factor instead:
      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        mutate(`CAUSE CODE` = as.factor(`CAUSE CODE`))

      # Now R treats it as a set of labels
      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$`CAUSE CODE`)
      #&gt;  [1] "1"  "4"  "6"  "7"  "8"  "9"  "10" "11" "14" "15" "16" "17" "19" "20" "21" "22"
      #&gt; [17] "25" "26" "28" "29" "30" "35" "36" "38" "39" "42" "44" "47" "50" "52" "54" "57"
      #&gt; [33] "58" "59" "60" "61" "63" "64" "66" "68" "69" "70" "71" "78" "79" "80" "81" "86"
      #&gt; [49] "92" "98"

      # And refuses to average them
      mean(kenya_accidents_2016$`CAUSE CODE`, na.rm = TRUE)
      #&gt; Warning in mean.default(kenya_accidents_2016$`CAUSE CODE`, na.rm = TRUE):
      #&gt;   argument is not numeric or logical: returning NA
      #&gt; [1] NA
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        That warning is R quite rightly refusing to do something meaningless.
        Converting <c>CAUSE CODE</c> from numeric to a factor is a claim about
        what the variable represents, and it deserves as much thought as any
        other analytical decision.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the decision comes down to what you're claiming is true about the
        variable.
        Is the difference between one value and the next a meaningful quantity,
        something you could add, subtract, or average?
        Then store it as a number.
        Are the values just different labels, with no numeric relationship
        between them?
        Then store it as an unordered factor.
        Do the values have a natural order that carries real information, where
        the gaps between levels aren't meaningful quantities?
        Then store it as an ordered factor.
        Get this wrong, and you can end up with results that are arithmetically
        fine, but analytically meaningless.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-factors-recoding">
      <title>When the categories change: Recoding factor levels</title>

      <p>
        Deciding on the levels of a factor is hardly ever a one-off decision.
        In practice, the categories you start with often aren't quite the
        categories you need for your analysis.
        Some levels might be too detailed, with so few observations in each that
        you can't make any meaningful comparisons.
        Others might reflect distinctions that matter for administration, but
        not for your analysis.
        And sometimes a classification scheme has changed over time, and you
        need to line up old labels with new ones.
      </p>

      <p>
        That last problem is one that analysts working with South African census
        data face directly.
        Under apartheid, the Population Registration Act imposed the racial
        classifications we described at the start of this chapter.
        After the transition to democracy, South Africa carried on collecting
        data on race, to measure apartheid's legacy and track progress towards
        equity.
        The 1996 census, the first one after apartheid, asked people to classify
        themselves using four categories: Black African, Coloured, Indian or
        Asian, and White.
        The 2001 and 2011 censuses used the same four categories, but with
        slightly different wording and ordering across datasets and survey
        instruments.
      </p>

      <p>
        So if you want to compare racial inequality across the 1996, 2001, and
        2011 censuses, you can't just join the three datasets and treat the race
        variable as if it were identical in all three.
        The labels need to be checked, lined up, and in some cases collapsed or
        recoded before any comparison makes sense.
        This is what recoding is about: adjusting the levels of a factor so they
        fit the question you're actually trying to answer.
        It means being deliberate and open about which distinctions you're
        treating as meaningful, and making sure the same word means the same
        thing in every dataset you're combining.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can see a simpler version of this in the Kenya accidents data.
        In <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, we created an <c>AGE_GROUP</c>
        variable from the raw <c>AGE</c> column, which had a mix of numeric ages
        and codes like <c>A</c> (adult) and <c>J</c> (junior), and turned it
        into two clean categories.
        That recoding used <c>case_when()</c>.
        Once we have the categories, we can make them an explicit factor, with a
        defined set of levels:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      kenya_accidents_2016 &lt;- kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        mutate(
          AGE_GROUP = case_when(
            AGE %in% c("A", "ADULT")           ~ "ADULT",
            AGE %in% c("J", "JUNIOR")           ~ "JUNIOR",
            suppressWarnings(as.numeric(AGE)) &gt;= 18 ~ "ADULT",
            suppressWarnings(as.numeric(AGE)) &lt; 18  ~ "JUNIOR",
            TRUE ~ NA_character_
          ),
          # Make it an explicit unordered factor
          AGE_GROUP = factor(AGE_GROUP, levels = c("JUNIOR", "ADULT"))
        )

      levels(kenya_accidents_2016$AGE_GROUP)
      #&gt; [1] "JUNIOR" "ADULT"

      table(kenya_accidents_2016$AGE_GROUP)
      #&gt;
      #&gt; JUNIOR  ADULT
      #&gt;     38    327
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        By setting <c>levels = c("JUNIOR", "ADULT")</c> ourselves, we control
        the order these categories appear in tables, plots, and model output.
        Without it, R would put them in alphabetical order (<c>ADULT</c> before
        <c>JUNIOR</c>).
        With it, we get the more natural order, from younger to older.
      </p>

      <p>
        The IPC food security classification shows a different kind of recoding
        decision, where levels are collapsed or combined to match the question
        being asked.
        A researcher studying the difference between households in Crisis and
        households in Emergency needs to keep those levels separate.
        A researcher asking simply <q>how many households are food insecure?</q>
        might reasonably collapse Crisis, Emergency, and Famine into a single
        level.
        Neither choice is wrong, but both need to be made on purpose, and
        reported clearly.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-splitting-by-factors">
      <title>What a factor actually buys you: Splitting data by group</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          So far, we've talked about what factors are, and how to define them
          carefully.
          But why does it matter?
          What does treating a variable as a factor actually let you do?
        </p>

        <p>
          One big answer is grouping.
          Once you've recognised a variable as a factor, you can split your data
          by it: working out a separate summary for each level, drawing a
          separate plot panel for each group, or fitting a separate model within
          each category.
          Every comparison between groups, every chart with one line per region
          or one bar per category, and every table that breaks results down by
          county or by IPC phase relies on treating the grouping variable as a
          factor, and respecting its levels.
        </p>

        <p>
          Here's a simple example using the Kenya accidents data.
          Now that <c>VICTIM</c> is a factor, we can count crashes by victim
          category in one line:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
      kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        group_by(VICTIM) %&gt;%
        summarise(crashes = n()) %&gt;%
        arrange(desc(crashes))
      #&gt; # A tibble: 19 × 2
      #&gt;    VICTIM                      crashes
      #&gt;    &lt;fct&gt;                         &lt;int&gt;
      #&gt;  1 PEDESTRIAN                      152
      #&gt;  2 M/CYCLIST                        82
      #&gt;  3 PASSENGER                        50
      #&gt;  4 DRIVER                           46
      #&gt;  5 P/PASSENGER                      26
      #&gt;  6 P/CYCLIST                         7
      #&gt;  7 MOTOR CYCLIST                     2
      #&gt;  8 RIDER                             2
      #&gt;  9 2 PASSENGERS &amp; DRIVER             1
      #&gt; 10 2 PEDESTRIANS &amp; PASSENGER         1
      #&gt; 11 4 PASSENGER 1 DRIVER              1
      #&gt; 12 7 PASSENGERS 1 DRIVER             1
      #&gt; 13 DRIVER PASSENGER                  1
      #&gt; 14 M/CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER           1
      #&gt; 15 MOTOR CYCLIST &amp; P/PASSENGER       1
      #&gt; 16 MOTOR CYCLIST P/PASSENGER         1
      #&gt; 17 P/PASENGER                        1
      #&gt; 18 PASSENGERS                        1
      #&gt; 19 PEDAL CYCLIST                     1
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          We can do the same by county, or by month, to see whether crashes peak
          in certain months:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
      kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
        group_by(month) %&gt;%
        summarise(crashes = n())
      #&gt; # A tibble: 2 × 2
      #&gt;   month crashes
      #&gt;   &lt;ord&gt;   &lt;int&gt;
      #&gt; 1 Apr       215
      #&gt; 2 Jun       163
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Notice that <c>month</c> is shown as <c>&lt;ord&gt;</c>, an ordered
          factor, and the months appear in calendar order, instead of
          alphabetical order.
          That order came from how we defined the factor, and not from the data.
          May doesn't appear at all because, as we saw in <xref
          ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, the dataset doesn't include any data for
          May.
          If you want every level to show up, even with zero crashes, you can
          add <c>.drop = FALSE</c> inside <c>group_by()</c>.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you look back, this is also exactly what happened in <xref
          ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>.
          At Berkeley, <c>department</c> was a factor in exactly this sense.
          The university-wide admission rate that looked like discrimination was
          calculated by ignoring that factor, and pooling every department into
          one group.
          Splitting by department meant grouping by the factor, and that one
          step made the real pattern visible, and reversed the conclusion.
          You can see this in the plot split by department in <xref
          ref="fig-berkeley-admissions-by-dept"/>.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-factors-grouping-payoff">
          <title>Grouping is what a factor is for</title>

          <p>
            A factor is much more than a column that happens to hold category
            labels.
            It's the thing you can split your data <em>by</em>.
            Get a factor's levels wrong, as the story at the start of this
            chapter shows, and every group you calculate is quietly the wrong
            group.
            Skip grouping by a factor that really matters, as <xref
            ref="ch-multilevel-data"/> showed, and you get one number that
            doesn't honestly describe any part of your data.
            So when you decide to treat a variable as a factor, you're really
            deciding which comparisons you'll be able to make.
          </p>
        </insight>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-example-flamingoes">
        <title>Example using Flamingoes Data</title>

        <p>
          The Kenya flamingo data gives us a really clear example of what
          splitting by a factor makes possible.
          The dataset has 344 birds, measured across three lakes (Bogoria,
          Elementaita, and Nakuru), two species (Greater Flamingo and Lesser
          Flamingo), and two sexes.
          Each of these is a natural factor to split on.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    library(ggplot2)
    library(dplyr)
    library(statsdata)

    data(kenya_flamingos)

    kenya_flamingos &lt;- kenya_flamingos %&gt;%
      mutate(
        lake    = factor(lake),
        species = factor(species),
        sex     = factor(sex, levels = c("female", "male"))
      )

    # A simple split: mean body mass by species
    kenya_flamingos %&gt;%
      group_by(species) %&gt;%
      summarise(mean_mass = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE))
    #&gt; # A tibble: 2 × 2
    #&gt;   species          mean_mass
    #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;                &lt;dbl&gt;
    #&gt; 1 Greater Flamingo     3120.
    #&gt; 2 Lesser Flamingo      1689.
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Greater Flamingos are nearly twice as heavy as Lesser Flamingos.
          But we have three factors we can split on (species, lake, and sex),
          and if we look at them one at a time, we miss how they interact.
          So let's build a plot that splits by all three at once.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    # Body mass by species and sex, faceted by lake
    kenya_flamingos %&gt;%
      filter(!is.na(sex), sex != "") %&gt;%
      ggplot(aes(x = sex, y = body_mass_g, fill = species)) +
        geom_boxplot(alpha = 0.7) +
        facet_wrap(~ lake) +
        scale_fill_manual(values = c("#F37021", "#44575E")) +
        labs(
          title = "Body mass of flamingos by sex, species, and lake",
          x = "Sex",
          y = "Body mass (g)",
          fill = "Species"
        ) +
        theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-flamingo-mass-split">
          <caption>
            Body mass of Greater and Lesser Flamingos by sex, faceted by lake.
            Three factors (species, sex, and lake) are encoded simultaneously
            using position, fill colour, and facet panel.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch7_flamingo_mass_split.png" width="100%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A set of three side-by-side boxplot panels, one per lake
                (Bogoria, Elementaita, Nakuru).
                Within each panel, body mass is shown on the y-axis with female
                and male on the x-axis.
                Two overlapping boxplots per x-axis position show Greater
                Flamingo (orange) and Lesser Flamingo (dark teal).
                Greater Flamingos are consistently heavier than Lesser Flamingos
                across all lakes and both sexes.
                Males tend to be slightly heavier than females within each
                species.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          We can also look at the relationship between bill length and body
          mass.
          Let's start with no split at all:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_flamingos %&gt;%
      filter(!is.na(bill_length_mm), !is.na(body_mass_g)) %&gt;%
      ggplot(aes(x = bill_length_mm, y = body_mass_g)) +
        geom_point(alpha = 0.6, colour = "#44575E") +
        geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE, colour = "#F37021") +
        labs(
          title = "Bill length vs body mass",
          x = "Bill length (mm)",
          y = "Body mass (g)"
        ) +
        theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-flamingo-bill-mass-nosplit">
          <caption>
            Bill length against body mass for all 344 flamingos, with no species
            split.
            A single trend line is fitted across all birds.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch7_flamingo_bill_mass_nosplit.png" width="80%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A scatter plot with bill length in millimetres on the x-axis and
                body mass in grams on the y-axis.
                All points are the same dark teal colour.
                The points form two loose clusters, one in the lower left and
                one in the upper right, but without colour coding this
                structure is not immediately obvious.
                A single orange trend line runs through all points, suggesting a
                moderate positive relationship between bill length and body mass
                across all birds.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          There's a positive trend (longer bills, heavier birds), but the points
          are really spread out, and the trend line is doing a lot of work over
          what looks like quite an awkward cloud of points.
          Something about this data doesn't quite fit a single straight line.
          Now let's add species as a factor, and map it onto colour and shape:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_flamingos %&gt;%
      filter(!is.na(bill_length_mm), !is.na(body_mass_g)) %&gt;%
      ggplot(aes(x = bill_length_mm, y = body_mass_g,
                 colour = species, shape = species)) +
        geom_point(alpha = 0.6) +
        geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE) +
        scale_colour_manual(values = c("#F37021", "#44575E")) +
        labs(
          title = "Bill length vs body mass by species",
          x = "Bill length (mm)",
          y = "Body mass (g)",
          colour = "Species",
          shape  = "Species"
        ) +
        theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-flamingo-bill-mass-split">
          <caption>
            The same data as <xref ref="fig-flamingo-bill-mass-nosplit"/>, now
            split by species.
            What looked like a single awkward cloud resolves into two tightly
            clustered groups, each with its own clear relationship between bill
            length and body mass.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch7_flamingo_bill_mass_split.png" width="80%">
            <description>
              <p>
                The same scatter plot as before, but points are now coloured
                orange for Greater Flamingo and dark teal for Lesser Flamingo,
                with different point shapes for each species.
                The two clusters are now clearly visible and entirely separate:
                Greater Flamingos in the upper right with longer bills and
                heavier bodies, Lesser Flamingos in the lower left.
                Each species has its own trend line, both showing tight positive
                relationships within the species that were obscured when all
                birds were plotted together.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The only thing that changed between the two plots is that in the
          second one, we treated <c>species</c> as a factor and used it to split
          the plot.
          What looked like one moderate trend across a big cloud of points turns
          into two tighter clusters, each with a clearer relationship between
          bill length and body mass.
          The overall trend was mostly being driven by the difference
          <em>between</em> the two species, rather than by the relationship
          <em>within</em> either of them.
          Without splitting by the factor, you wouldn't know which of those two
          things you were looking at.
          This is exactly the kind of structural question we met in <xref
          ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>: if you ignore a grouping variable, it's
          still there, just hidden inside your summary statistic.
        </p>

        <p>
          All of these summaries and plots needed <c>species</c>, <c>lake</c>,
          and <c>sex</c> to be treated as factors, so that <c>ggplot2</c> could
          split the data on them cleanly.
          The factor sets the boundaries, and the plot shows you what's inside
          them.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can go a step further and add sex, to see whether the relationship
          between bill length and body mass is different for male and female
          birds within each species:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
    kenya_flamingos %&gt;%
      filter(!is.na(bill_length_mm), !is.na(body_mass_g),
             sex %in% c("female", "male")) %&gt;%
      ggplot(aes(x = bill_length_mm, y = body_mass_g,
                 colour = sex, shape = species)) +
        geom_point(alpha = 0.6) +
        geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE) +
        scale_colour_manual(values = c("female" = "#F37021",
                                       "male"   = "#44575E")) +
        labs(
          title = "Bill length vs body mass by species and sex",
          x     = "Bill length (mm)",
          y     = "Body mass (g)",
          colour = "Sex",
          shape  = "Species"
        ) +
        theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-flamingo-bill-mass-sex">
          <caption>
            Bill length against body mass for flamingos, split by both species
            (point shape) and sex (colour).
            Orange points and lines are female; dark teal are male.
            Circles are Greater Flamingos; triangles are Lesser Flamingos.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch7_flamingo_bill_mass_sex.png" width="90%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A scatter plot with bill length in millimetres on the x-axis and
                body mass in grams on the y-axis.
                Points are coloured orange for female and dark teal for male,
                and shaped as circles for Greater Flamingo and triangles for
                Lesser Flamingo.
                Within Lesser Flamingos, males are slightly heavier than females
                across the bill length range, and both show a weak positive
                trend.
                Within Greater Flamingos, the male trend line slopes downward:
                birds with longer bills tend to be lighter, while the female
                trend is roughly flat.
                This reversal within Greater Flamingos is a surprising finding
                that would be invisible without the sex split.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          And something unexpected shows up.
          Within Lesser Flamingos, males are slightly heavier than females
          across the whole range of bill lengths, and both sexes show a weak
          positive trend (longer bills, slightly heavier birds).
          Nothing surprising there.
          But within Greater Flamingos, the male trend line slopes
          <em>downward</em>: male Greater Flamingos with longer bills tend to be
          lighter.
          The female trend is roughly flat.
          This reversal, where the direction of a relationship changes depending
          on which group you look at, is exactly what we called Simpson's
          paradox in <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>.
          The overall trend across all Greater Flamingos (in <xref
          ref="fig-flamingo-bill-mass-split"/>) suggested a positive
          relationship.
          Split by sex, that trend reverses for males.
        </p>

        <p>
          Is this real biology?
          Perhaps males with longer bills use their energy differently, or
          perhaps longer-billed males tend to be older and lighter.
          Or is it just an artefact of a small sample of Greater Flamingos?
          That's a question an ornithologist is much better placed to answer.
          What splitting by the factor does is make the pattern visible in the
          first place.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 4: Exercises              -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-handling-factors">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-factors-identify">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each of these variables, would you store it as a number, an
          unordered factor, or an ordered factor?
          Briefly explain why.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The cause code in the Kenya road accidents data.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The IPC food security classification for a household.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The county in which a crash occurred.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The year a crash occurred.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The hour of the day a crash occurred.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-factors-create-ordered">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the Kenya road accidents data, pull the hour of day out of the
          <c>TIME_PARSED</c> column (which we created in <xref
          ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>), and store it as an ordered factor.
          Then use <c>group_by()</c> and <c>summarise()</c> to count the number
          of crashes in each hour.
          Does the order of the factor change the output?
          What would happen if you used an unordered factor instead?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-factors-recode-victim">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <c>VICTIM</c> column in the Kenya road accidents data has several
          levels that mean the same thing, like <c>M/CYCLIST</c> and <c>MOTOR
          CYCLIST</c>, or <c>P/PASENGER</c> and <c>P/PASSENGER</c>.
          Use <c>fct_recode()</c> or <c>case_when()</c> to collapse these into
          clean levels.
          Then make a bar chart of the counts for each victim category.
          How does the chart change compared with using the uncleaned factor?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-factors-ipc-collapse">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The IPC food security classification has five levels: Minimal,
          Stressed, Crisis, Emergency, and Famine.
          IPC treats Crisis or worse as <term>acute food insecurity</term>.
          A researcher wants to report simply what proportion of households are
          in acute food insecurity (Crisis, Emergency, or Famine), and what
          proportion aren't (Minimal or Stressed).
          Use <c>fct_collapse()</c> from the <c>forcats</c> package to create a
          new two-level factor from the five-level IPC variable.
          What information do you lose by collapsing the levels like this, and
          when might that be justified?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-factors-south-africa">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-factors-recoding"/> describes the challenge of comparing
          South African census data across 1996, 2001, and 2011, where the race
          variable was worded slightly differently in different surveys.
          Suppose the 1996 census used the level <c>"Indian or Asian"</c>, and
          the 2001 census split this into separate <c>"Indian"</c> and
          <c>"Asian"</c> levels.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              What would happen if you appended the two datasets without
              recoding first?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Write R code using <c>fct_recode()</c> or <c>case_when()</c> to
              standardise both datasets to use a single consistent set of levels
              before appending.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What assumption are you making when you collapse <c>"Indian"</c>
              and <c>"Asian"</c> back into <c>"Indian or Asian"</c>?
              Is that assumption defensible?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>